<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puʻukoholā Heiau National Historic Site</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Puʻukoholā Heiau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puʻukoholā <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">Heiau</a> National Historic Site, at Kawaihae on the northwestern coast of the island of Hawaiʻi, preserves the ruins of the last major ancient Hawaiian temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> built the luakini, the 'Temple on the Hill of the Whale', on the advice of the priest Kapoukahi to win the favour of the war god Kūkaʻilimoku; thousands of people raised it by hand without mortar, passing stones along a human chain about 14 miles long, and it was finished in the summer of 1791. His rival Keōua Kuahuʻula, invited there for peace, was killed on landing and his body offered to dedicate it. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pu-ukohola-heiau-national-historic-site.jpg" alt="Puʻukoholā Heiau National Historic Site" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bamse · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pu'ukohola_Heiau_temple2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> took control of the Kona and Kohala districts of western and northern Hawaiʻi island in 1782, but fought inconclusive battles for the next eight years. After he returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1790 he was attacked by his cousin Keōua Kuahuʻula, who still held the east of the island, and he went back to the village of Kawaihae. The temple stands probably on the site of an older one of about 1580. Its red stones came from Pololū Valley, and the work was supervised by Kamehameha's brother Keliʻimaikaʻi.</p><p>In 1790 the ship Fair American was captured with one surviving crew member, Isaac Davis. He and the stranded British sailor John Young became military advisers to Kamehameha, trained his army in the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> on double-hulled canoes. With these the forces of the other islands were repelled at the Battle of Kepuwahaʻulaʻula, just north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waipio-valley">Waipiʻo Valley</a>.</p><p>The finished temple measured about 224 by 100 feet. Why Keōua accepted the invitation is not clear: he may have been discouraged by the loss of many warriors in the Battle of Hilo and the volcanic eruption of 1790, and one story holds that he mutilated himself in secret to defile the sacrifice. The killing became known as the Slaughter at Kawaihae. Kamehameha went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1794 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> in 1795, and in 1810 the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, making him the first king of a unified Hawaiʻi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The National Park Service runs a visitor centre, from which a trail leads to the temple; the public may not enter it, as bones are believed still to lie there. About 170 feet to the west is the ruin of the earlier Mailekini Heiau, which John Young turned into a fort to guard the harbour, and the site of Young's house and ranch is also in the park. Just offshore lies Hale o Kapuni, an underwater structure dedicated to sharks. The site was listed in the National Register of Historic Places on 15 October 1966, and its name took the Hawaiian spelling in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puʻukoholā Heiau National Historic Site” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
